## 4. Handwriting pages sealed on finalize (M3.3 PR2)

1. **The seal.** `MedicalRecordService::finalize()` — after the visit lock, the sheet lock and every finalization
   check, inside the same transaction — calls `MedicalRecordHandwritingRepositoryInterface::sealBookPages()`: EVERY
   page of the patient's handwriting book (page 1 in `trx_medical_record_handwritings`, pages 2+ in
   `trx_medical_record_handwriting_pages`, on every sheet of the patient) that is not sealed yet gets `sealed_at`
   (technical instant, UTC) and `sealed_by_medical_record_id` (the sheet finalized), the live encounter's own pages
   included. An existing seal is never re-stamped; another patient's pages are never touched. The seal columns are
   not mass-assignable; only the repository writes them, through the base query (the row's `updated_at` keeps
   meaning "last drawn").
   - *Why there is no exception.* The first version of this section spared the live encounter's pages when the sheet
     finalized was not that encounter's own. The M3.3 PR2 review (HIGH-1) proved that this left a returning
     patient's book completely unsealed: the book is created during the live encounter on the patient's CANONICAL
     (earliest) visit (R7-1-FS), so finalizing it — the patient's only sheet — spared every page that encounter wrote,
     and those pages of a FINAL sheet stayed overwritable then and in later encounters. The owner's rule is literal:
     when a sheet is finalized, the pages are sealed. The live encounter keeps APPENDING new pages (§4.4).
2. **The refusal.** `MedicalRecordHandwritingService::save()` — under the visit lock and the record lock, AFTER the
   page-revision compare (rule 237 §15, a stale save still answers stale) and BEFORE any object is written — refuses
   an overwrite of an existing page the live encounter may not overwrite (§4.3, §4.4), key `handwriting_data`:
   `MESSAGE_SEALED_PAGE` for a sealed (or pre-rule covered) page, `MESSAGE_EARLIER_ENCOUNTER_PAGE` for a page another
   encounter wrote or an unstamped page no finalize covers (§4.4). Nothing is written: no object, no row. It binds every caller, Super Admin included
   (`Gate::before` lets Super Admin past `MedicalRecordPolicy::update`, which cannot see a page anyway). Refusal
   order with the PTM gate (rule 241 §2; contrast §1.8): a page save is consent-gated as a whole — the controller asks
   the authority before validation and again inside the save, before the record lock the seal is decided on — so on
   an encounter whose PTM is missing an overwrite of a sealed page answers with the consent refusal. That is
   deliberate: the only write the book then allows, a NEW page, needs the PTM too. With the PTM executed the seal
   answers (`MedicalRecordFinalizationPageSealTest`).
3. **Sealed** (`pageSlotLock()` → `LOCK_SEALED`): a page row that is sealed; or a row with neither a seal nor an
   authoring stamp (written before this rule) when a final sheet of the patient was finalized at or after the row's
   `created_at` — a tie locks, a missing `created_at` or a final sheet without `finalized_at` locks, a soft-deleted
   final sheet still counts. No backfill: such rows are judged by this derived rule until a finalize seals them; until
   then §4.4 locks them against overwrite.
4. **Only the live encounter overwrites its own pages; everyone appends** (plan default 4, "a page after the encounter
   only as an amendment item", accepted 2026-11-17). An existing page may be overwritten only when it is not sealed
   AND its `authored_clinic_visit_id` is the live encounter (the authorized encounter the controller resolved,
   `RmeVisitConsentService::assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()`). A page another encounter wrote — even one never
   sealed because that encounter finalized nothing — is refused (`LOCK_EARLIER_ENCOUNTER`). A page with no author
   stamp (written before this rule) is sealed under §4.3 when a finalize covers it, and otherwise is refused as
   `LOCK_EARLIER_ENCOUNTER` too (review MEDIUM-A): the encounter that wrote it was never recorded, so it is never the
   live encounter's own, and letting a later encounter overwrite it would restamp a closed encounter's page as that
   encounter's. The accepted cost: an encounter that spans the deploy loses overwrite of its own pre-deploy page and
   keeps appending. `save()` requires the encounter: a resolver that names none is a programming error, refused
   with a `LogicException` before any object or row is written (review LOW-B), so no unstamped row is written after
   this rule; `pageLockMessage()` with no live encounter locks every existing page. A slot with no row (a new page 1 of an empty book, or the next page number) is never
   locked: a live, consented encounter keeps adding pages to the one book (OD-5-6, R7-1 — stored on the book's
   visit). Each write stamps `authored_clinic_visit_id` with the live encounter, because `clinic_visit_id` names the
   book's visit, not the encounter.
5. **Lock order (extends §1.4).** Finalize: visit → sheet → page rows (the seal UPDATE). Save: visit → book record
   (`lockForHandwriting`) → page read. Both are consent-gated writes of one patient, so they serialise on the
   encounter's visit row.
6. **The page mirrors the server (rule 135).** `MedicalRecordController::show()` asks
   `MedicalRecordHandwritingService::pageLockMessage()` for the active native page with the live encounter; a page
   the live encounter may not overwrite renders read-only (`data-sealed="1"`, no "Klik untuk menulis", the service's
   reason). The controller also prepares the editor's target: on such a page the shared editor posts only to the
   next new page of the book, and "+ Tambah Halaman RM" stays where the server allows it.
7. **Migration.** `2026_11_17_200001_add_seal_columns_to_handwriting_page_tables` adds `authored_clinic_visit_id`,
   `sealed_at` and `sealed_by_medical_record_id` (nullable, foreign keys restrict on delete, with explicit names
   `trx_mrh_*` / `trx_mrhp_*` — the generated ones exceed PostgreSQL's 63 characters) to both page tables. Additive
   only (CG-09); no existing row is touched.
8. **Supersedes** the Sprint 59 comment "handwriting RM is revisable after finalization" in
   `MedicalRecordHandwritingController` and the corresponding part of rule 109 §2b's last bullet (already marked by
   §2).

## 5. Version history (M3.3 PR3)

1. **The table.** `trx_rme_record_versions` (migration `2026_11_17_300001_create_trx_rme_record_versions_table`,
   additive): one row per version of a subject — `subject_type` (`medical_record` | `odontogram`) and `subject_id`,
   `version` (UNIQUE per subject, ≥ 1), `kind` (`final` | `baseline` | `amendment`), `snapshot_schema_version`,
   `snapshot` (`json`, never `jsonb` — §5.6), `previous_hash`, `hash`, `created_by`, `created_at` (technical instant,
   UTC; no `updated_at`). PostgreSQL CHECK constraints pin the subject types, kinds and version ≥ 1.
2. **Immutable.** `RmeRecordVersion` throws on update and delete; on PostgreSQL the row trigger
   `trx_rme_record_versions_immutable` refuses a raw UPDATE or DELETE, and the statement trigger
   `trx_rme_record_versions_no_truncate` (same function) refuses a TRUNCATE, which no row trigger sees (SQLite has
   no trigger and no TRUNCATE; the model guard stands). The repository has no update or delete. `down()` refuses to
   drop the table while a version exists.
3. **Version 1.** `MedicalRecordService::finalize()` and `OdontogramService::finalize()` call
   `RmeRecordVersionService::recordFinal()` inside their transaction, after the visit and subject locks (and, for a
   sheet, after the page seal of §4), so the version commits with the finalize or not at all. The already-final
   paths return before it, and `recordFinal()` itself returns an existing version 1, so a re-finalize writes nothing.
   A version is written only inside a transaction and only for a final sheet or a finalized chart (LogicException
   otherwise).
4. **Baseline.** A record finalized before rule 240 has no version. `RmeRecordVersionService::ensureBaseline()`
   writes its version 1 (`baseline`) from the record as it stands, once; M3.3 PR5 calls it inside the amendment
   transaction, under the same locks, before the record changes, naming the amending (live) encounter so that
   encounter's own unsealed pages — still writable under §4.4 — are not recorded as locked.
5. **The snapshot.** A sheet: `fields` (subjective, objective, assessment, plan, notes), `diagnoses` (code system,
   code, display, role, statuses, notes, diagnosed at; by id), `diagnosis_overrides` — the emergency overrides of
   the structured-diagnosis requirement (`trx_diagnosis_requirement_overrides`: id, reason, `used_by` as an account
   id, `expires_at`, `created_at`; by id), part of the locked sheet (§1.1) and possibly what let it finalize — and
   `pages` — every LOCKED page of the patient's handwriting book by the rule of §4.3-§4.4 (sealed; written before
   rule 240 with no authoring stamp, covered by a finalize or not; or written by an encounter other than the live
   one — `MedicalRecordHandwritingRepositoryInterface::lockedBookPages()` lists a row exactly when `pageSlotLock()`
   answers a lock for it; at a finalize every page has just been sealed, so it is the whole book), with its
   record, page number, object key and SHA-256 (never bytes; an inline data-URI page reports no key), authoring
   encounter and seal stamps. A chart: summary notes, additional conditions and `tooth_map_payload`. Both carry the
   subject's ids, status, `finalized_at` and `finalized_by`. No patient identity field is copied; nothing is
   logged and no audit row carries a snapshot.
6. **The hash chain.** `hash` = SHA-256 of the canonical JSON (object keys sorted recursively, lists in order, the
   flags of `RmeInvoiceSnapshot`) of subject type, subject id, version, kind, `previous_hash`,
   `snapshot_schema_version` (since M3.3 PR5, before any release: a snapshot read under the wrong schema is detected)
   and snapshot; version
   1 has no previous hash, version n+1 carries version n's hash. The snapshot column holds the snapshot's canonical
   JSON text verbatim — a `json` column, whose text PostgreSQL returns exactly as written (SQLite: text) — and the
   model stores only that text (no `array` cast). The hash is computed over the envelope with that exact stored text
   spliced in as the snapshot (`RmeRecordVersion::snapshotJson()`), never over a re-encoding of its decoded value, so
   nothing between write and verify can change what is hashed. Why not `jsonb` and a decode/re-encode (review
   MEDIUM-C): `jsonb` re-encodes the text and PHP's decode/encode does not round-trip every number — a chart payload
   `5.0` was hashed as `5.0` and stored by the `array` cast as `5`, so `verifyChain()` failed for a legitimate row
   (and `-0.0` reads back as `0` or `0.0`); normalising numbers on both sides would have to anticipate every such
   difference, while hashing the stored bytes needs none. `verifyChain()` checks numbering 1..n, each link and each
   stored hash against the stored text and stored schema version. Every version is written by one private writer
   (`RmeRecordVersionService::write()`), so the stored text and the hash come from the same `canonicalJson()` result.
7. **Reading and verifying.** `versionsFor()` and `version()` authorize the subject's `view` ability for the given
   account (MedicalRecordPolicy / OdontogramPolicy) before reading; the version history pages are §7. REVISED IN
   PART by rule 243 §2.12 (doctor encounter scope, flag ON only): a sheet is asked `viewAnchorContent`
   (`RmeRecordVersionService::readAbility()`), here and on every amendment surface, and a version page lists only
   the locked pages the reader's page routes would serve. The chain is
   verified where it matters: `approve()` refuses (`MESSAGE_CHAIN_BROKEN`, nothing written) to chain a new version
   onto a history that does not verify, and the version page shows a warning (`data-version-chain-broken`) when it
   does not. The amendment detail page mirrors `approve()` (rule 135): for a pending amendment on a chain that does
   not verify it withholds "Setujui dan Terapkan" (`actionsFor()` `can_approve` false, `chain_broken` true) and shows
   the same warning above the unverified "before"; rejecting stays possible. The list page reads no snapshot and does
   not verify.
8. **The "Diamandemen n×" marker.** `amendmentCount()` counts the subject's `amendment` versions. No marker column
   is added: a counter would have to be written beside every amendment and could drift from the immutable rows it
   summarises; the count reads one indexed set (`subject_type`, `subject_id`, `kind`) per printed record.
9. **Page objects are never deleted.** A handwriting save writes every page to a fresh object key and never removes
   the object a committed row names; the only removal in that path is the uncommitted object of a failed save
   (`MedicalRecordHandwritingService::discardUncommitted()`), whose key no row references. A version therefore
   always points at objects that still exist. No other code path deletes handwriting objects (the public-disk
   migration command removes a public copy only after a byte-identical private copy is verified) — except the same
   rule-224 compensation for an amendment's correction page (§8.3: `RmeAmendmentService` removes the object of a
   request whose body failed, a fresh key no row names); an applied correction page's object is the page's object.

## 6. Supervised amendment — request, cancel, reject (M3.3 PR4)

1. **The table.** `trx_rme_amendments` (migration `2026_11_17_400001_create_trx_rme_amendments_table`, additive): the
   subject (`subject_type` = `RmeRecordVersion::SUBJECT_*`, `subject_id`), its `branch_id` and `clinic_visit_id`
   (copied from the subject by the service, never from a request; the patient is read through the visit,
   `clinicVisit.patient` — there is deliberately no `patient_id`: a patient merge reassigns
   `trx_clinic_visits.patient_id` and visit ids stay valid, so the amendment follows the surviving patient, while a
   patient column would be a reference `PatientOwnedRecordRegistry` does not know — blocking every merge — that the
   row guard could never let a merge rewrite), `base_version_id` /
   `base_version` (the version the request was made against), `status`, `reason` (10–1000 characters),
   `proposed_content` (`json`, read back in the order written), `requested_by`, `cancelled_at`, `decided_by`,
   `decided_at`, `decision_reason`, timestamps (technical instants, UTC; pages render WITA). Constraint names
   `trx_rme_amd_*` (≤ 63 characters). A partial unique index `trx_rme_amd_one_pending_uq` (both engines) keeps one
   `pending` amendment per subject. PostgreSQL CHECKs: subject types, statuses, `base_version >= 1`, the reason
   bounds, maker ≠ checker `trx_rme_amd_maker_checker_chk` (`decided_by IS NULL OR decided_by <> requested_by`), and
   the shape of each status (pending: no `decided_by`, `decided_at`, `cancelled_at` or `decision_reason`; cancelled:
   `cancelled_at` and none of the decision columns; rejected/applied: `decided_by` and `decided_at`, no
   `cancelled_at`; rejected: a bounded `decision_reason`). The PostgreSQL trigger `trx_rme_amendments_guard` (BEFORE
   UPDATE OR DELETE per row, BEFORE TRUNCATE per statement) refuses any change of the request columns
   (`RmeAmendment::REQUEST_COLUMNS`: subject, branch, visit, base version, reason, proposal compared as
   text, requester, `created_at`), any change of a row that left `pending`, and every DELETE or TRUNCATE; the model
   refuses the same on every engine, and `status` is not mass-assignable (a new row is always `pending`). Because
   `base_version_id` references `trx_rme_record_versions`, a plain TRUNCATE of the version table now fails on the
   foreign key, and `TRUNCATE … CASCADE` on the triggers. `down()` refuses while a row exists.
2. **State machine.** `pending` → `cancelled` (the requester), `rejected` (a checker, with a written reason) or
   `applied` (M3.3 PR5: approve and apply in one transaction). Every other transition is refused ("no longer
   pending"). A cancel sets `cancelled_at` and never `decided_by`, so the CHECK holds.
3. **Requester (plan default 2).** First the account must be able to `view` the record (MedicalRecordPolicy /
   OdontogramPolicy) — otherwise "not found" (404) before anything else, the reason's length included, so a
   stranger never learns whether the record is a draft or final. Then `RmeAmendmentService::requestRefusal()`, in order: the subject is a final sheet or
   a finalized chart; the account holds `request_rme_amendments`; it IS the doctor of record (the sheet's
   `doctor_id`, for a chart the chart visit's `doctor_id`, → `mst_doctors.user_id`) or the finalizer
   (`finalized_by`). A chart's doctor of record is the visit's CURRENT `doctor_id`: a reassignment of the visit
   moves it. Identity, not role: a Super Admin who is neither is refused, one who finalized the record may request.
   A finalizer without the permission (e.g. a Supervisor RME) may not request. A doctor row with no linked account
   leaves only the finalizer (a departed doctor's record waits).
4. **Request.** `RmeAmendmentService::request()` bounds the reason (trimmed), asks the requester rule, then inside
   one transaction: locks the subject's visit (FOR NO KEY UPDATE), then the subject row (sheet: FOR NO KEY UPDATE via
   `lockForHandwriting`; chart: FOR UPDATE via `findByClinicVisitForUpdate`), re-decides the requester rule on the
   locked row, refuses a second pending amendment, validates the proposal against the locked record, takes the
   latest version as the base (or `ensureBaseline()` for a record finalized before this rule, naming the patient's
   live encounter from `ActiveEncounterResolver`), and inserts the row inside a savepoint: a unique violation of
   `trx_rme_amd_one_pending_uq` (a raced check) is rolled back to the savepoint and refused cleanly with the
   "already pending" message. Nothing in the record changes; the §1 lock keeps refusing direct edits.
   - *Sheet proposal:* `fields` — only the SOAP fields and notes (`SHEET_FIELDS`), line endings normalised, empty =
     null; only the fields that differ (surrounding whitespace ignored, as `TrimStrings` posts them) are stored,
     and only a changed field is bounded (≤ 10 000 characters). Every value is type-checked before it is cast: an
     array role (`MESSAGE_DIAGNOSIS_ROLE`), array notes or a non-digit id (`MESSAGE_DIAGNOSIS_ROW`) answer 422.
     `diagnoses` — `remove` (integer ids of THIS sheet's diagnoses) and `add` (≤ 10 rows with only
     `clinical_diagnosis_id`, `diagnosis_role`, `notes`: an ACTIVE master, a valid role, notes ≤ 500, not already on
     the sheet unless removed in the same proposal; an untouched form row naming no diagnosis is ignored); at most
     one primary after the change. The state after a diagnosis change must pass the finalization gate
     (`DiagnosisRolloutService::assertFinalizationAllowed()`): on a `pilot_enforced` branch an active primary or a
     usable override must remain (`MESSAGE_PRIMARY_REQUIRED`); M3.3 PR5 must re-check it when applying.
   - *Chart proposal:* the whole proposed chart (`summary_notes`, `additional_conditions`, `tooth_map_payload`;
     an absent key keeps the current value). The chart AFTER the change — absent keys filled from the current
     chart — is what is validated (M3.3 PR5 review), so a request is never accepted that no approval could apply;
     a null tooth map is refused only when it would erase one. Validated with the rules of a chart save — the rules of
     `UpdateOdontogramPlaceholderRequest` are the source of truth (fields, statuses, conditions, lengths), plus FDI
     numbers, no duplicate condition, only `teeth` in the tooth map and only the per-tooth keys that request
     validates (every tooth an object). Sizes are therefore bounded like a chart save (52 FDI teeth, fixed
     per-field lengths); a tooth's `conditions` is a list of distinct known values (at most the number the save
     rule allows, no null, never a map — as the editor writes it, `MESSAGE_CONDITIONS`). An explicit null, an
     unreadable tooth map, or one without a `teeth` object (`{}`, `{"teeth": null}`) is refused
     (`MESSAGE_TOOTH_MAP`): it cannot be told apart from a broken editor, and clearing a whole chart is not an
     amendment intent (an empty chart is `{"teeth": {}}`); the HTTP form requires the editor's tooth map and keeps
     an unreadable string as it came, so the `array` rule refuses it. The controller passes the posted proposal
     (`RmeAmendmentActionRequest::proposal()` = the input, never `validated()`, whose excludeUnvalidatedArrayKeys
     would drop the notes beside the validated tooth map) and the service validates all of it; after a refusal the
     form restores the posted teeth.
   - Unknown keys are refused at every level, and so is a proposal that changes nothing (for a chart: nothing that
     the before/after view would show as changed). Performed treatments and prescriptions are not amendable (plan
     default 5); a handwriting correction page rides with a sheet amendment (§8, M3.3 PR6).
5. **Cancel.** `cancel()` locks visit → subject → amendment (FOR UPDATE) and refuses unless the amendment is pending
   and the actor IS its requester (binds Super Admin).
6. **Reject (plan defaults 1 and 6).** `reject()` bounds the decision reason, then inside the transaction: the
   account holds `approve_rme_amendments` (Supervisor RME; Super Admin through Gate::before); visit → subject →
   amendment are locked; the amendment's branch is inside the account's scope (`RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()`)
   and the account may `view` the subject — otherwise "not found" (404); the amendment is pending; the account is
   not the requester (`MESSAGE_SELF_DECISION`), not the doctor of record (`MESSAGE_DOCTOR_OF_RECORD`) and not the
   finalizer of the record (`MESSAGE_FINALIZER`, creator ≠ approver, CG-07), all decided on the locked rows,
   binding Super Admin. The same refusals hide the decision controls (`actionsFor()`), and M3.3 PR5's approval
   inherits them. A stale base version is not checked on reject (nothing is
   written to the record); M3.3 PR5 must refuse an approval whose base is no longer the latest version.
7. **Three layers and the pages.** Routes (`rme.amendments.*`): `permission:request_rme_amendments|approve_rme_amendments`
   and `cache.headers:private;no_store;max_age=0` on the group; the queue and reject need `approve_rme_amendments`,
   the request form, store and cancel need `request_rme_amendments`. `RmeAmendmentPolicy` (registered in
   RepositoryServiceProvider): `viewAny` (approve permission), `view` (the requester, or an approver whose branch
   scope covers it, and who may view the subject — else `denyAsNotFound`), `request`, `cancel`, `reject` (each asks
   visibility first and answers `denyAsNotFound`, then gives the service's own refusal).
   `RmeAmendmentActionRequest::authorize()` runs before validation and answers 404 to an account that may not
   `view` the record (store) or the amendment (decisions), so an out-of-scope account never gets a validation
   answer. The controller is thin and calls `assertMayRequest()` before the form and the store, so a Super Admin
   who is neither gets 403 there too. The supervisor queue lists the approver's list scope
   (`RmeWorkingBranchScope::resolve()`), pending first; the patient's name, medical record number and the link
   show only on a row the approver may open (`viewableIds()` — an approver may be narrowed by the doctor-patient
   scope). The detail page shows the base version beside the proposal (a chart tooth by tooth, with every field each
   side stores and the changed rows marked — `chartDiff()`) and only the actions `actionsFor()` allows (approve-and-apply
   since M3.3 PR5, §7.7). The final sheet and chart
   pages render an "Amandemen" block (`RmeAmendmentService::panelFor()`: pending status, recent amendments, links
   only for an account that may open them) and "Ajukan Amandemen" only when the service would accept this account
   now (rule 135 mirror). The sidebar link is gated by `approve_rme_amendments`.
8. **Audit.** `sys_audit_logs`, entity `trx_rme_amendments`: `RME_AMENDMENT_REQUESTED` (subject, branch, base
   version and id, status, `reason_length`), `RME_AMENDMENT_CANCELLED` (subject, branch, status),
   `RME_AMENDMENT_REJECTED` (subject, branch, status, `requested_by`, `decision_reason_length`). Ids and lengths
   only — never the reason, the decision reason or the proposed content (CG-11). Database notifications to the
   approvers move to M3.3 PR5 (the `app/` file budget of this PR).
9. **Tests (§3).** Request by the doctor of record and by the finalizer; another doctor, a Super Admin who is neither
   and a finalizer without the permission refused; draft refused; reason bounds; the proposal rules; base = latest
   version, a baseline only when none exists; one pending (service, and the partial unique index raced through a
   repository double — refused inside a savepoint, the connection still usable); cancel and reject matrices
   (requester, doctor of record also holding Supervisor RME, Super Admin on their own request refused, otherwise
   allowed); the lock still refusing direct edits; audit ids-only; the PostgreSQL CHECK refusing
   `decided_by = requested_by` on a raw insert, the constraint names, visit → sheet → amendment order; the HTTP role
   matrix (Front Office, Kasir, Admin Klinik, Perawat, a treating doctor who is neither, Supervisor RME: 403; a
   doctor who never treated the patient: 404 on a draft and on a final record alike); a context-bound approver
   seeing only its branch (queue, detail and reject 404 outside it, also with an invalid decision reason); a
   requester seeing only their own amendment; `private, no-store`; the sheet and chart pages offering the request
   only to the doctor of record. Review fixes: a Supervisor RME or Super Admin finalizer refused as checker (service
   and HTTP, no decision control); an unreadable, empty or null tooth map refused (an absent one keeps the chart);
   unknown keys refused in the tooth map, a tooth and a diagnosis row; the chart detail rendering every tooth field
   with the changed row marked; the last primary not removable on a `pilot_enforced` branch; only a changed field
   bounded; `status` not mass-assignable; the model and, on PostgreSQL, the trigger refusing edits of the request,
   any change of a decided row and DELETE; the tightened status CHECKs; queue identity hidden from an approver
   outside the patient scope. Re-review: a chart notes-only change and a notes-and-teeth change round-trip through
   the form, and every part of a sheet proposal; `{"teeth": null}`, `{}`, a conditions map, a null condition or
   too many conditions refused; the posted teeth restored after a refusal; a chart finalizer refused as checker
   (service and HTTP); TRUNCATE of `trx_rme_amendments` refused on PostgreSQL; "not found" before the reason;
   refusals asserted by their message constants (`MESSAGE_UNKNOWN_PART`, `MESSAGE_DIAGNOSIS_NOT_ON_SHEET` — also
   for an id with trailing junk —, `MESSAGE_MASTER_INACTIVE`, `MESSAGE_SECOND_PRIMARY`, `MESSAGE_TOOTH_MAP_PART`,
   `MESSAGE_TOOTH_NUMBER`, `MESSAGE_TOOTH_FIELD`, `MESSAGE_CONDITIONS`, and the §1 lock constants on a live
   encounter).
10. **Deploy.** The two permissions are new: run `PermissionSeeder`, then `RoleSeeder`, then
    `permission:cache-reset` (CG-15). Doctor gains `request_rme_amendments`; Supervisor RME gains
    `approve_rme_amendments`; Super Admin through '*'.
11. **Outside the PTM gate (owner decision 2026-11-17, "AMENDMENT — APPROVE: No PTM gate, record it", CG-22).** A
    supervised amendment corrects a record that already exists, so it stays OUTSIDE the PTM gate (rule 241 §2.3):
    `request()`, `cancel()`, `reject()`, `approve()` and `actionsFor()` never consult the consent. What stays: the
    append-only versions and the immutable original (§5), the doctor as requester (§6.3), a separate Supervisor RME
    as approver (§6.6, §7), the written reason and the audit trail (§6.8). The original PTM state is RECORDED for
    information: `RME_AMENDMENT_REQUESTED` and `RME_AMENDMENT_APPLIED` carry `visit_ptm_state`, the amended visit's
    PTM at that moment as its persisted consent evidence records it — `executed`, `patient_only` (the patient's
    signature, no doctor countersignature), `none`, or `voided` (no live consent, a voided one in its history); a
    code, no personal data (`RmeAmendmentService::ptmCodeOf()`, from `RmeVisitConsentService::ptmStateFor()` and
    `historyFor()`). The detail page shows, read-only, the visit's CURRENT recorded PTM and, when the state recorded
    at request differs (e.g. a supervised void in between), that one beside it; an amendment requested before the
    audit carried it shows the current state only. `RmeAmendmentService::detailFor()` decides both (`original_ptm`:
    `state`, `at_request`); the Blade only renders them (`data-amendment-original-ptm`,
    `data-amendment-original-ptm-at-request`). No decision reads either value. No consent row is created, changed or
    back-dated for an amendment, and a historical consent is never made up. A NEW medical act is not an amendment:
    it takes the examination workflow and the PTM. Tests: `tests/Feature/RME/MedicalRecordFinalizationAmendmentPtmStateTest.php`
    — a sheet and a chart amendment requested, shown and applied in each of the four states, both audits carrying
    the state and every consent row of the visit unchanged; a supervised void between request and decision shown as
    both values, the apply audit recording `voided` and the request audit `executed`; a re-signed visit names its
    live consent, not the voided one.

## 7. Approve and apply (M3.3 PR5)

1. **One transaction.** `RmeAmendmentService::approve()` approves AND applies, so the checker approves exactly what
   is applied. It locks the subject's visit (FOR NO KEY UPDATE), the subject row and the amendment (FOR UPDATE),
   then decides on those rows, binding Super Admin: the account holds `approve_rme_amendments`; the amendment's
   branch is in its scope and it may `view` the record (otherwise "not found", 404); `decisionRefusal()` — the
   amendment pending, not the requester, not the doctor of record, not the finalizer (§6.6); the subject still
   final; the subject's LATEST version is still the amendment's `base_version_id` (otherwise `MESSAGE_STALE_BASE`:
   the requester files a new request). An optional note (≤ 1000 characters) is the `decision_reason`.
2. **Re-validation.** The stored proposal is validated again against the locked record under today's rules — the
   sheet field allowlist and bounds, diagnosis ownership, ACTIVE masters, at most one primary, the
   `pilot_enforced` finalization gate on the state after the change (§6.4); a chart through the chart-save rules
   — and must come out identical to what was requested (`MESSAGE_PROPOSAL_CHANGED` otherwise). A refusal names its
   reason under the `amendment` key.
3. **Apply.** Only this path writes a final record; the §1 lock stays and keeps refusing every normal edit
   (`updateDraft`, the diagnosis writes, the chart save) after an apply. A sheet: the changed fields through
   `MedicalRecordRepositoryInterface::update()`; the diagnosis change through
   `RmeAmendmentRepositoryInterface::applySheetDiagnoses()` (removed rows soft-deleted, this sheet's only; added
   rows diagnosed by the requester, created by the approver). A chart: the whole proposed chart through
   `OdontogramRepositoryInterface::updatePlaceholder()`. Status, `finalized_at` and `finalized_by` never change.
   `applySheetDiagnoses()` itself refuses (LogicException) outside a transaction or on a sheet that is not final.
   **SATUSEHAT:** every apply refreshes the visit's existing SATUSEHAT candidate after commit, so drift revokes an
   approval (`source_changed`, `revoked_at`, review back to pending) — the conservative direction; it decides no
   re-submission policy (gateway disabled; that policy is an open owner decision). A saved sheet row is refreshed
   by `SatusehatMedicalRecordObserver` (after commit, so it reads the diagnoses written by the same apply);
   `RmeAmendmentService` refreshes, as `MedicalRecordDiagnosisService::refreshCandidate()` does, after a
   diagnosis-only apply, after a chart apply (the chart feeds the dental readiness) and whenever that observer is
   off (`satusehat.candidate.auto_generate`) — never twice, because a second refresh would overwrite the
   `source_changed` readiness of the first. Not run: the per-diagnosis SATUSEHAT audit events of
   `MedicalRecordDiagnosisService`.
4. **The next version.** `RmeRecordVersionService::recordAmendment()` writes version n+1 (`amendment`) of the
   changed record, `previous_hash` = the base version's hash, through the one writer (§5.6); it refuses unless the
   given version is still the latest. The snapshot's pages name the patient's live encounter, as the baseline
   does (§5.4).
5. **The amendment row.** One UPDATE moves it `pending` → `applied` with `decided_by`, `decided_at`,
   `decision_reason` and the link to the version it wrote — `applied_version_id` (unique, foreign key on
   PostgreSQL) and `applied_version` — set in that same statement (the §6.1 guard refuses any later change of an
   applied row; `base_version_id` never changes). Migration `2026_11_17_500001_add_applied_version_to_trx_rme_amendments_table`
   (additive): the two columns, `trx_rme_amd_applied_version_uq`, and on PostgreSQL `trx_rme_amd_applied_version_fk`,
   `trx_rme_amd_applied_chk` (`applied` ⇔ both set) and `trx_rme_amd_applied_version_chk` (≥ 2). The foreign key is
   PostgreSQL-only because adding one on SQLite rebuilds the table and would turn the partial unique index of
   §6.1 into a full one.
6. **Audit and notifications.** `RME_AMENDMENT_APPLIED` (subject, branch, status, `requested_by`, base version,
   applied version and id, `decision_reason_length`) — ids and lengths only. Database notifications
   (`RmeAmendmentNotification`, the `RmeBillingDisputeNotification` pattern: in-app only, payload `title`,
   `message`, `url`, `rme_amendment_id`): to every usable account that could decide a new request (approve
   permission, scope, none of the §6.6 refusals — evaluated in a rolled-back transaction, as the dispute
   escalation does), and to the requester on a reject and on an apply. Sent after commit; a failed one is logged
   with ids only and changes nothing.
7. **Markers and pages.** "Diamandemen n×" (`amendmentCount()`, the applied amendments counted from the versions)
   on the RME print and PDF (`ClinicVisitPrintDataBuilder`, shared with the oversight print), on the odontogram
   print (`OdontogramPrintFormatter::format()['amendment_count']`, shared with the oversight odontogram print; the
   counts of all the patient's finalized charts are read in one grouped query per formatter, so the odontogram
   history list does not count per chart),
   and in the "Amandemen" block of the final sheet and chart pages, with a link to the version history. Version
   history: `rme.amendments.versions.sheet|chart` (list: number, kind, author, time — no snapshot, authors
   eager-loaded) and `...show` (one version beside the previous one), behind
   `permission:view_clinic_visits|manage_clinic_visits` and `private, no-store`; the record's own `view` policy
   decides (404 outside its scope). Route keys: `{medicalRecord}` and `{odontogram}` are governed and constrained
   globally (AIR-RMC-37 — the per-route `whereNumber` of M3.3 PR4 is removed, it widened the global pattern);
   `{amendment}` takes `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN` per route and `{version}` `DatabaseRouteKey::PAGE_NUMBER_PATTERN`
   (a 1-based int4 number), so an out-of-range segment answers 404, never 500. A sheet version shows its fields (changed ones marked), diagnoses,
   emergency overrides and its locked pages — each only as the existing policy-gated, no-store page route
   (`rme.handwritings.image` for page 1, `rme.handwriting-pages.image` for pages 2+), never an object key; a chart
   version shows the §6 tooth-by-tooth diff against the previous version. The detail page offers "Setujui dan
   Terapkan" only when `actionsFor()` says the service would accept it (`can_approve`: a checker, and the base
   still the latest version; a stale amendment says why).
8. **Concurrency.** Two approvals, an approval after a cancel, an approval after a reject: each decides on the
   locked amendment row, so a stale copy is refused ("no longer pending") and nothing is written twice; the
   version number is also UNIQUE per subject. Proven in-process with stale model copies and the PostgreSQL lock
   order (visit → sheet → amendment → the record write); a separate probe connection cannot see the test's own
