## 8. Handwriting amendment page (M3.3 PR6)

1. **Owner decision (2026-11-17, "BUILD AMENDMENT PAGE FIRST", §0.3).** A doctor who may request an amendment of a
   final sheet (§6.3: doctor of record or finalizer, holding `request_rme_amendments`) may attach ONE new
   handwriting correction page to it, with the amendment's written reason. A Supervisor RME approves (§7, maker ≠
   checker through `decisionRefusal()`). On approval the page is appended to the patient's book as a NEW sealed
   page. An original page — its row and its object — is never changed, overwritten or deleted. Charts carry no
   correction page (`MESSAGE_CORRECTION_PAGE_SHEET_ONLY`).
2. **Schema.** Migration `2026_11_17_600001_add_correction_page_to_rme_amendments_and_handwriting_pages` (additive):
   `trx_rme_amendments.correction_page_path` (the object key; `$hidden` on the model, never rendered),
   `correction_page_sha256`, `corrects_page_number` (optional); `trx_medical_record_handwriting_pages.rme_amendment_id`
   (nullable, `trx_mrhp_rme_amendment_fk` restrict on delete, unique `trx_mrhp_rme_amendment_uq` — one page per
   amendment). PostgreSQL CHECKs: `trx_rme_amd_correction_page_chk` (key and SHA-256 together, 64 lowercase hex,
   a corrected page only with a page and ≥ 1), `trx_rme_amd_correction_subject_chk` (a sheet only),
   `trx_mrhp_rme_amendment_sealed_chk` (an amendment page is sealed). The three amendment columns are REQUEST
   columns (`RmeAmendment::REQUEST_COLUMNS`): the model refuses a change on every engine, and the migration
   replaces `trx_rme_amendments_guard()` with the §6.1 body plus exactly these three comparisons (down() restores
   it) — a pending row may still be cancelled, rejected or applied, nothing else of the request changes. The
   unreleased PR4 migration is not edited. Only pages 2+ get the link: the amendment page is always the book's NEXT
   page (`nextPageNumber()` ≥ 2), never page 1. Because the pages table now references `trx_rme_amendments`, a plain
   TRUNCATE of it fails on the foreign key and `TRUNCATE … CASCADE` on the §6.1 trigger (the PR4 TRUNCATE test
   was flipped to pin both, as §5.2's version-table test was in PR4).
3. **Request** (`RmeAmendmentService::request(…, $correctionPage, $correctsPageNumber)`). After "not found", the
   reason bounds and the requester rule, and BEFORE anything is written, the canvas is decoded by
   `HandwritingPayloadGuard::decodeCanvas()` — the one canvas decoder, moved unchanged from
   `MedicalRecordHandwritingController` (payload length, `data:` prefix, strict base64, PNG magic, a 13-byte IHDR —
   on every canvas path since the review INFO, a shorter one used to answer 500 from the blank check — rule 237 §5
   declared-size and inflate bounds, the Sprint 59.1 blank guard) — then held to `assertCleanPng()` (review L1): the
   signature, the 13-byte IHDR first, every chunk complete with a valid CRC and on an ALLOWLIST — `IHDR`, `PLTE`,
   `IDAT`, `IEND` and only the colour-space, density and transparency chunks a canvas export can carry (`sRGB`,
   `gAMA`, `cHRM`, `pHYs`, `iCCP`, `tRNS`); any other chunk, whatever its case bits (`tEXt`, `tEXT`, `htMl`, …), is
   refused. Every chunk but IDAT appears at most once and holds exactly what its type defines (re-review LOW-1):
   `IEND` 0 bytes, `sRGB` 1, `gAMA` 4, `cHRM` 32, `pHYs` 9; `PLTE` a multiple of 3 up to 768 and only in a palette
   image; `tRNS` 2 bytes for grey, 6 for RGB, 1–256 for a palette, never beside an alpha channel; `iCCP` ≤ 64 KiB, a
   1–79-byte printable profile name, compression method 0 and ONE zlib stream ending at the chunk's end (≤ 1 MiB
   inflated). The IHDR declares a valid colour type and bit depth, compression and filter method 0 and NO interlacing
   (a canvas export never is). The IDAT chunks are consecutive and followed only by IEND, nothing after IEND; and
   their concatenated data is ONE zlib stream that ends exactly at its last byte (nothing after the stream end) and
   inflates to exactly height × (1 + row bytes), every scanline opening with a filter type 0–4 (re-review LOW-2: a page
   with no image data, data that is not a zlib stream or does not decode to the declared image is refused, never
   sealed — the blank guard alone counts undecodable data as content). So nothing rides along OUTSIDE the image. What
   remains is image content — the pixels and a palette, where any byte value is a colour — so the page is also served
   only as `image/png` with `nosniff`, never as markup. These checks run on the amendment path only (request and
   approval), never on the ordinary handwriting save. Refusals are keyed `handwriting_data` (`MESSAGE_INVALID`,
   `MESSAGE_TOO_LARGE`, `MESSAGE_BLANK`). A named page without a drawing is refused
   (`MESSAGE_CORRECTS_PAGE_WITHOUT_PAGE`). Inside the transaction, after visit → sheet locks, the requester
   re-decision and the one-pending check: the proposal may be empty when a page rides with it (a page-only
   amendment, stored as `[]`; `allowEmpty` also at apply); `corrects_page_number` must name a page of the patient's
   BOOK holding a stored drawing (`MESSAGE_CORRECTS_PAGE`; page 1 is the legacy row, 2+ the pages table) that the
   AMENDED ENCOUNTER wrote (review M2, `MESSAGE_CORRECTS_PAGE_ENCOUNTER`): a stamped page's `authored_clinic_visit_id`
   is the amended sheet's visit; a page from before rule 240 (no stamp) counts only when the amended sheet sealed it
   (`sealed_by_medical_record_id`). The request form lists exactly those pages, by book number ("Halaman buku RM N").
   Then, as one unit (rule 224): the object is written to
   `ClinicalEvidenceStorage` under a fresh server key `handwritings/{branch}/{visit}/amendment_{YmdHis}_{16 hex}.png`
   (the client never supplies one), then the row (in the savepoint of §6.4) with the key and the SHA-256 of the
   bytes. A failure in the body — the raced one-pending index included — removes this call's object and only it; a
   failure after the body (COMMIT, after-commit callbacks) keeps it, because the row may be committed. A second
   pending request is refused before its object is written.
4. **Approve and apply.** Inside §7's single transaction, after the visit → sheet → amendment locks, the checker
   rules, the stale-base check, the chain verification (§5.7, `MESSAGE_CHAIN_BROKEN` — so a broken history is never
   extended by a page or a version) and the proposal re-validation: the object is read back by
   `HandwritingPayloadGuard::storedPng()` (present, a PNG, SHA-256 equal) — a missing or tampered object answers
   `MESSAGE_CORRECTION_PAGE_INTEGRITY`, a storage read fault `MESSAGE_CORRECTION_PAGE_UNREADABLE` ("coba lagi", no
   new request — review L2), and nothing is written — and re-run through today's canvas rules
   (`MESSAGE_PROPOSAL_CHANGED`); then the patient's book (`PatientRmWorkspaceResolver::canonicalMedicalRecord()`,
   R7-1 — the record new pages are written on; the amended sheet when the patient has no book in the RME branches) is
   locked with `lockForHandwriting()` — the record lock a page save takes — so the lock order is **visit → sheet →
   amendment → book → page insert → version insert**; the named page must still be in the book and still the amended
   encounter's own (`MESSAGE_PROPOSAL_CHANGED` otherwise). After the sheet's
   text and diagnoses are applied, `MedicalRecordHandwritingRepositoryInterface::appendSealedAmendmentPage()` (only
   inside a transaction) inserts the page as the book's next page on the book's visit, with the pending key and
   SHA-256, `authored_clinic_visit_id` = the amended sheet's visit (the encounter), `sealed_at` = now,
   `sealed_by_medical_record_id` = the amended sheet, `rme_amendment_id`, `created_by` = the requester (who drew it),
   `updated_by` = the approver. Sealed at once: `pageSlotLock()` answers `LOCK_SEALED`, so no encounter — the live one
   included — overwrites it (§4.2), and a later finalize never re-stamps it. The UNIQUE(record, page) index is the
   backstop for the number. The approval data (approver, time) is the amendment's `decided_by` / `decided_at`.
   `recordAmendment()` then writes the next version, whose snapshot lists the page through `lockedBookPages()` (object
   key, SHA-256 and — on an amendment page only — `rme_amendment_id`; every other page is described exactly as
   before, so earlier snapshots and hashes are unchanged); `verifyChain()` stays true. A page-only apply changes
   nothing SATUSEHAT reads, so it refreshes no candidate.
5. **Display and print.** The label (`RmeAmendment::correctionPageLabel()`, null until applied, review M3) links back
   to what it corrects: `Halaman koreksi — amandemen #id untuk kunjungan <nomor> (<tanggal> WITA)[, mengoreksi halaman
   buku RM N]; disetujui <d/m/Y H:i> WITA oleh <nama>`. It shows in the RM workspace (the active page,
   `data-amendment-page`, beside its book number "Halaman buku RM N" — the workspace's own index may differ), the RME
   print (`MedicalRecord::orderedHandwritingPages()` adds `amendment_id` to an amendment page only; its label is
   §9.5's, per viewer) and the version history (`versionDetailFor()`). The amended sheet's page (the "Amandemen" block, `panelFor()`
   `correction_pages`) and its print carry, beside "Diamandemen n×", one pointer per applied correction page:
   `Halaman koreksi: buku RM halaman N (amandemen #id)` (`MedicalRecord::correctionPagePointers()`). Page numbers on
   the form and the detail page are the book's ("halaman buku RM N"). Images go only through policy-gated,
   `private, no-store` routes, never an object key: the workspace and version pages link
   `rme.handwriting-pages.image`, which serves an amendment page only when `storedPng()` verifies it (404 for a
   missing or tampered object, 503 for a storage read fault, an ids-only warning either way); the print embeds only
   verified bytes, else "Gambar halaman ini tidak tersedia.". The approver sees the pending page before approval on
   the detail page through `rme.amendments.correction-page` (route permission
   `request_rme_amendments|approve_rme_amendments`, `RmeAmendmentPolicy::view` — the requester or an approver in
   scope, 404 otherwise — and `RmeAmendmentService::correctionPageImage()`, verified, `nosniff`; 404 missing or
   tampered, 503 unreadable); the approval is not offered unless the object verifies (rule 135 mirror). The detail
   banner tells a read fault (`data-correction-page-unreadable`, retry) apart from tampering
   (`data-correction-page-tampered`), and says "tidak dapat diterapkan" only while the amendment is pending (review
   L3).
6. **Rejected or cancelled.** The pending object is kept (committed evidence is never deleted, §5.9) and stays on
   the amendment's own page, marked "tidak diterapkan"; it never becomes a book page (no row links it).
7. **Audit (ids and digests only, CG-11).** `RME_AMENDMENT_REQUESTED` adds `correction_page_sha256` and
   `corrects_page_number`; `RME_AMENDMENT_APPLIED` adds `correction_page_id`, `correction_page_number`,
   `correction_page_medical_record_id` and `correction_page_sha256`. Never the key, the bytes or the image.
8. **Three layers.** Store: the §6.7 route permission, `RmeAmendmentActionRequest::authorize()` (404 before any
   validation for an account that may not see the record), `handwriting_data` bounded at the edge (string, ≤
   `MAX_HANDWRITING_PAYLOAD_LENGTH`) and `corrects_page_number` an int4 ≥ 1, the policy's `request`, and the
   service deciding everything again on the locked rows (binding Super Admin). Approve: §7 unchanged.
9. **Tests (§3).** Request: the private object under a fresh server key with key and SHA-256 on the row, no key in
   the row's array or the audit; non-PNG, SVG, GIF, non-base64, HTML after IEND, a script in a text chunk, a broken
   CRC, oversized dimensions and payload, a blank canvas — all refused with nothing written; the corrected page
   validated against the book (and refused without a page); a chart refused; the requester matrix (Super Admin who
   is neither, a finalizer without the permission, a doctor who may not see the record — no object written; the
   doctor of record and a permitted finalizer accepted). Apply: the page appended as the next page, sealed, stamped
   and linked, the original rows and bytes unchanged, an overwrite through `MedicalRecordHandwritingService::save()`
   refused as sealed, version 2 listing it with key and SHA-256 (the baseline not), `verifyChain()` true, the
   audit; a page-only amendment; a later sheet appending to the patient's book stamped with its own encounter; a
   tampered or missing object refused with nothing written; a broken version chain refused before any page or
   version is written (`MESSAGE_CHAIN_BROKEN`); the checker matrix (requester, finalizer, Super Admin on
   their own request) appending nothing; the corrected page bound to the amended encounter in the form, at request
   and at approval, the unstamped-page rule included (review M2); a storage read fault told apart from tampering at
   apply, on the image route (503) and on the detail page (review L2). Concurrency: two approvals append one page; an approval after a cancel or a
   reject appends none and keeps the object; a second request refused before writing; the raced one-pending index
   refused in the savepoint with this call's object removed; rule 224 compensation keeping the earlier evidence.
   Canvas (review L1, INFO): `tEXT` and `htMl` chunks, an oversized `iCCP` and IDAT split around another chunk
   refused; a short IHDR refused as a validation answer by `decodeCanvas()` (every canvas path), never a 500; a canvas
   carrying `sRGB`, `gAMA`, `pHYs`, a small `iCCP` and split consecutive IDATs accepted. Canvas (re-review LOW-1,
   LOW-2): a payload in `IEND`, `pHYs`, `sRGB`, `gAMA`, `cHRM` or `tRNS`, a `tRNS` beside an alpha channel, a palette
   beside an alpha channel, an oversized palette, a raw `iCCP` payload or one after its zlib stream, bytes after the
   IDAT zlib stream, a chunk twice and a chunk after the image data refused; no image data, data that is not a zlib
   stream, a stream cut before its end, a row short or too many, an unknown filter type, interlacing, an impossible
   bit depth and a palette image without `PLTE` refused; what libpng (through GD) writes — 900 × 1273 RGBA and RGB,
   a palette with `tRNS`, `pHYs` beside each — and the RGBA stream in Chromium's IDAT layout (4096-byte chunks and
   a last 6-byte one) accepted. The book page route answers 503 on a storage read fault (review L2). Display: the label (encounter,
   corrected page, approval) and the pointer, verified print bytes, a tampered page printed as unavailable. Constraints: the model guard
   on the three columns, one page per amendment; on PostgreSQL the trigger refusing raw changes, the CHECKs and the
   constraint names, and the lock order visit → sheet → amendment → book → page → version. HTTP: front desk 403, a
   treating doctor who is neither and Super Admin 403, a stranger 404 before the canvas is validated, form errors
   for a bad canvas or page number with nothing written; the pending image private, no-store, `nosniff`, to the
   requester and an in-scope approver only (out-of-scope approver: show, image, approve and reject 404; stranger 404;
   front office 403), a tampered one 404 with no approval offered; after approval the label in the workspace, the
   print (with the embedded bytes) and the version page, no object key anywhere, the page route serving verified
   bytes and refusing a tampered object, the print saying so, an applied amendment's tampered banner without "tidak
   dapat diterapkan" (L3), the pointer beside "Diamandemen 1×" on the sheet's page and print, book numbering on the
